\section*{Strict convexity and differentiability of the exponential planar passage shape}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a mathematical result relevant to this question in the selected manuscript. The exponential planar first-passage shape is claimed to be strictly convex with a continuously differentiable boundary; Gamma laws are also claimed to give differentiable time constants.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For nearest-neighbor first-passage percolation on \(\mathbb Z^2\) with independent exponential weights of fixed rate \(\lambda>0\), let \(\mu\) denote the time-constant norm and \(\mathcal B=\{x:\mu(x)\le1\}\). The source claims differentiability of \(\mu\) on \(\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}\), a \(C^1\) boundary of \(\mathcal B\), and
\[
 x,y\in\partial\mathcal B,\ x\ne y,\ 0<t<1
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mu((1-t)x+ty)<1.
\]
Thus its unit ball is strictly convex and has one supporting line at each boundary point. This theorem is for exponential weights, not arbitrary passage laws.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The argument separates the removal of corners from the removal of flat faces. Busemann and geodesic comparisons establish differentiability for Gamma weights, while an additional exponential-law argument excludes linear boundary segments; the two conclusions are then combined.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The source gives strict convexity and a continuously differentiable passage-shape boundary for exponential edge weights of every positive rate. Rate one is the model requested here, so that specialization proves the target.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 212, the documented entries give differentiability and continuously differentiable boundaries for planar Gamma shapes, including the exponential law. They do not supply strict convexity or the separate no-bigeodesic theorem. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{GammaPassage.lean}, \texttt{PlanarFirstPassage.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.PlanarFPP.manuscriptMain}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

